response:
 First, notice $f(n) f(m + 1) f(n-m-1) = f(n+1) f(m) f(n-m).$ Plugging  $n=2$ into this gives \[f(2)\left(\frac{f(1)^2}{f(0)} + f(1)\right) = f(3) f(1)\]which equals \[f(2)\left(2^2 + 1\right) = 5 f(2) = 2 f(3).\]Thus we have $f(3) = 5,$ and plugging again yields \[f(4)\left(f(1)\frac{5}{f(0)} + \frac{1}{f(1)}\right) = f(5) f(1)\]which gets us $f(5) = 30.$ We notice we have $f(n)=\tfrac{n!}{(n-2)!}$ for prime $n$ so we have no doubt $f(n)=n$ for primes.  Plugging in $m = n-1$ gives \[f(n)\left(\frac{f(1)}{f(0)} + 1\right) = (n-1)f(n)\]and so \[f(n) + f(1) = (n-1)f(n)\]which equals \[\frac{2}{n-1}=1\]implying we must have $n=3,$ confirming our definition. If $n>3$ so we have $f(n) = n.$ It is easy to see this works</think>

