Human: Calculate the pH and the percentage dissociation of a 0.125 M solution of a monoprotic acid with a {eq}K_a

{/eq} of {eq}8.75 \times 10^{-3}

{/eq}.

Assistant: The equilibrium expression for the dissociation of a monoprotic acid is:

{eq}K_{a}=\dfrac{x^{2}}{C-x}

{/eq}

where C is the initial concentration of the acid.

Solving for x, we get:

{eq}x=\sqrt{K_{a}C}= 0.0296 \ \textrm{M}

{/eq}

Therefore, the concentration of {eq}\left [ H_3O^{+}\right ]  = 0.0296 \ \textrm{M}

{/eq}

{eq}\textrm{pH}= -\log \left [ H_3O^{+}\right ] = -\log (0.0296 ) = 1.53

{/eq}

The percentage dissociation is:

{eq}\textrm{Percentage dissociation}=  \dfrac{\sqrt{K_{a}C}}{C} \times 100 =  \dfrac{0.0296}{0.125} \times 100 = 23.68 \%  {/eq}

